{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a significant figure in the Crusader States of the Levant during the 12th century. *Early Life and Ascension to Power*: He was born in 1135 and succeeded his brother, Baldwin III, as Count of Tripoli in 1152. His youth was marked by challenges, including the loss of his father, Raymond II, and the struggle to maintain control over his territory.\n\n*Challenges and Achievements as Count*: Raymond III faced numerous threats to his rule, including invasions by the Nur ad-Din, the Seljuk Sultan of Rum. In 1157, he formed an alliance with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus to counter these threats. The alliance was pivotal in securing the Crusader States against the forces of Nur ad-Din. Furthermore, Raymond played a crucial role in the defense of the Principality of Antioch, demonstrating his strategic acumen and leadership.\n\n*Political and Military Leadership*: Raymond III was not only a capable military leader but also a shrewd politician. He maintained close ties with the Byzantine Empire and other Crusader leaders, which helped to stabilize the region. His reign saw significant developments in Tripoli, including the construction of fortifications and the promotion of trade. These efforts contributed to the economic growth and security of the county.\n\n*Death and Legacy*: Raymond III died in 1187, shortly after the Battle of Hattin, where the Crusader forces suffered a devastating defeat. His death marked the end of an era in the Crusader States. Despite the challenges he faced, his leadership left a lasting impact on the political and military landscape of the Levant. His strategic alliances and defense of Crusader territories are remembered as significant contributions to the history of the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.5496468544006348}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.000243